WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:02.544
- A different ANA presentation.

00:00:02.544 --> 00:00:07.090
Marketer, entrepreneur, social
media guru, internet personality

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:08.967
and agency owner.

00:00:08.967 --> 00:00:14.014
Your next speaker is not afraid
to tell it like he sees it.

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:16.600
Fresh out of college, he took
his family wine business and

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:21.309
grew from $3 million to 
$60 million in just five years.

00:00:21.309 --> 00:00:23.688
From there, he set his 
sights on marketing,

00:00:23.688 --> 00:00:26.109
social media
and entrepreneurship.

00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:29.821
With us to discussed the notion
of marketing in the year we live

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:33.486
in is the CEO of digital agency

00:00:33.486 --> 00:00:35.875
VaynerMedia, Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:00:35.875 --> 00:00:39.113
- [Gary] Thank you so much.
(audience applause)

00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:40.314
Thank you.

00:00:40.314 --> 00:00:41.892
Thanks for having me.

00:00:41.892 --> 00:00:44.985
(energetic music)

00:00:48.362 --> 00:00:49.368
Morning.

00:00:49.368 --> 00:00:50.523
(single man cheers)

00:00:50.523 --> 00:00:54.471
(laughs) I'm really
excited to be here.

00:00:54.471 --> 00:00:57.099
As somebody who has comes from
outside the industry over the

00:00:57.099 --> 00:01:01.270
last seven years I've become
very fond of my contemporaries

00:01:01.270 --> 00:01:05.022
and the creative and strategic
people in this industry and

00:01:05.022 --> 00:01:07.067
I've enjoyed it quite a bit.

00:01:07.067 --> 00:01:08.657
My back story is very immigrant.

00:01:08.657 --> 00:01:10.487
I was born in the Soviet Union.

00:01:10.487 --> 00:01:13.532
I came to the U.S.

00:01:13.532 --> 00:01:14.741
I lived in a studio apartment

00:01:14.741 --> 00:01:17.661
a fourth of the size of 
this stage with my family.

00:01:17.661 --> 00:01:20.414
My dad got a job as a
stock boy in a liquor store.

00:01:20.414 --> 00:01:23.750
We were super poor the first
six, seven years of being here.

00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:25.335
And eventually, how many people

00:01:25.335 --> 00:01:28.171
are immigrants or
children of immigrants?

00:01:28.171 --> 00:01:29.506
Raise your hand.

00:01:29.506 --> 00:01:31.843
Awesome. So you guys, 
a lot of you know.

00:01:31.843 --> 00:01:34.928
There's a really hard-core
secret immigrants have.

00:01:34.928 --> 00:01:37.597
It's a tried and true strategy.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.767
Don't spend money on dumb shit.

00:01:40.767 --> 00:01:42.853
(audience laughter)

00:01:42.853 --> 00:01:45.397
That's what my parents 
did for first 5 to 10 years.

00:01:45.397 --> 00:01:46.832
We saved and eventually my dad

00:01:46.832 --> 00:01:50.694
bought a small liquor store
in Springfield, New Jersey.

00:01:50.694 --> 00:01:52.362
This industry is so
interesting to me.

00:01:52.362 --> 00:01:56.009
Besides the last political
question, most of the questions

00:01:56.009 --> 00:01:59.161
that were asked of all these
wonderfully smart people the

00:01:59.161 --> 00:02:03.285
part that I was most fascinated
by as I watched that was who

00:02:03.285 --> 00:02:05.500
gets to be the judge and jury?

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:08.319
Right? Who gets to 
be the judge of quality?

00:02:08.319 --> 00:02:11.058
Who gets to be the judge and 
jury has been the number one

00:02:11.058 --> 00:02:13.672
question that I've had since 
I've entered this industry.

00:02:13.672 --> 00:02:16.435
'Cause, you see,
my story is simple.

00:02:16.435 --> 00:02:18.582
When you go into a family
business and you're a first

00:02:18.582 --> 00:02:22.341
generation immigrant and you
have to work for two bucks an

00:02:22.341 --> 00:02:25.301
hour as a 14-year-old in a
liquor store, there is no

00:02:25.301 --> 00:02:28.951
Cannes Lion Award that justifies
your marketing strategy.

00:02:28.951 --> 00:02:31.865
There is no modeling mix. Right?

00:02:31.865 --> 00:02:33.472
There's no subjectivity.

00:02:33.472 --> 00:02:35.744
My KPI was to sell
more Pinot Noir.

00:02:35.744 --> 00:02:37.359
If my marketing worked,

00:02:37.359 --> 00:02:39.323
my family ate more 
and had more stuff.

00:02:39.323 --> 00:02:43.580
And so, I come from a background
where I disproportionately

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:48.360
respect sales and marketing in
very separate silos but I find

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:51.927
myself in the last decade where
a lot of people mix the two and

00:02:51.927 --> 00:02:56.765
debate the two and don't
have as much vested interest as

00:02:56.765 --> 00:03:00.560
entrepreneurs and business
to actually dissect the two.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:05.190
What I'm excited to talk
about this morning is deploying

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.848
dramatic common sense in a

00:03:07.848 --> 00:03:10.698
2017 world to an industry that

00:03:10.698 --> 00:03:13.527
I've had an unbelievable
time getting to know.

00:03:13.527 --> 00:03:16.925
When I first got here, I came
out of building a liquor store

00:03:16.925 --> 00:03:20.147
from $3 to $60 million in
five years with no money.

00:03:20.147 --> 00:03:21.720
How did I do that?

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.420
You have to make
every penny work.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:25.422
Not only was it 
doing $3 million a year,

00:03:25.422 --> 00:03:28.133
we were running 
on 10% gross profit.

00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:29.968
So we had $300,000 before

00:03:29.968 --> 00:03:33.263
expenses to make 
our business grow.

00:03:33.263 --> 00:03:37.517
I did that because while Sherry,
Lehmann's and Zachy's and Sam's

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:42.105
and K&amp;amp;L and Wine.com with it's
$188 million in funding were

00:03:42.105 --> 00:03:44.983
doing the Wine Spectator and 
the New York Times and

00:03:44.983 --> 00:03:47.861
Wall Street Journal and
direct-mail and outdoor,

00:03:47.861 --> 00:03:50.948
I decided that launching an
e-commerce site in 1996 was a

00:03:50.948 --> 00:03:53.533
good idea instead of
opening a second store,

00:03:53.533 --> 00:03:55.744
this was going to be
my second and third store.

00:03:55.744 --> 00:03:57.120
The other thing I did was

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.289
I started an email 
newsletter in 1996.

00:03:59.289 --> 00:04:02.125
How many people here have
done or have been close to email

00:04:02.125 --> 00:04:03.085
marketing in their career?

00:04:03.085 --> 00:04:04.503
Please raise your hand.

00:04:04.503 --> 00:04:05.921
Great, you're going
to love this one.

00:04:05.921 --> 00:04:09.424
In 1996, 200,000 people 
on my email newsletter and

00:04:09.424 --> 00:04:11.927
I had 91.3% open rates.

00:04:11.927 --> 00:04:13.387
(audience groans and laughter)

00:04:13.387 --> 00:04:16.490
- Not because I was a genius
or the copy was so great.

00:04:16.490 --> 00:04:19.541
It was 'cause nobody
was email marketing.

00:04:19.541 --> 00:04:22.294
The one thing I promise you more
than anything, more than I know

00:04:22.294 --> 00:04:26.089
that the sun will come up
tomorrow I know one true fact:

00:04:26.089 --> 00:04:29.301
marketers ruin everything.

00:04:29.301 --> 00:04:31.762
(audience laughter)

00:04:31.762 --> 00:04:33.555
It's what we do.

00:04:33.555 --> 00:04:36.558
Two-and-a-half years ago I was
so pumped when I decided that

00:04:36.558 --> 00:04:39.102
Snapchat was going to be the
next frontier and I couldn't

00:04:39.102 --> 00:04:41.010
wait to fuck up
that little ghost.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:42.940
(audience laughter)

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.399
You wanted to 
follow your friends,

00:04:44.399 --> 00:04:47.194
I wanted to sell you shit.
Right?

00:04:47.194 --> 00:04:48.987
So that's been my life.

00:04:48.987 --> 00:04:50.280
Email was this new thing.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.283
I was like wait a minute, I can
reach people instead of doing

00:04:53.283 --> 00:04:56.082
direct mail and 
paying for the paper

00:04:56.082 --> 00:04:58.262
and paying for the stamp,

00:04:58.262 --> 00:05:01.515
I can do this and wait a minute
I was selling the wine Opus One,

00:05:01.515 --> 00:05:04.226
Chateau Lafite three to four
months ahead of my competition

00:05:04.226 --> 00:05:07.729
because between the time they
have to make the catalog and get

00:05:07.729 --> 00:05:10.274
it into your home, 
I was already in their inbox

00:05:10.274 --> 00:05:11.233
and here's the punchline.

00:05:11.233 --> 00:05:14.444
My friends, I am not a
digitalist or a futurist or

00:05:14.444 --> 00:05:16.935
definitely not a
social media guru.

00:05:16.935 --> 00:05:20.325
What I am is I really
give a shit about one thing.

00:05:20.325 --> 00:05:21.368
I have one pitch.

00:05:21.368 --> 00:05:23.954
If you're a baseball fan, 
I basically equate my career

00:05:23.954 --> 00:05:26.748
much more to Mariano Rivera 
than anything else.

00:05:26.748 --> 00:05:30.043
He was the Yankees closer for
15 years, he had one pitch and

00:05:30.043 --> 00:05:31.295
nobody could hit it.

00:05:31.295 --> 00:05:33.881
I've played the same game
since I was six years old.

00:05:33.881 --> 00:05:36.258
When I started my
entrepreneurial career,

00:05:36.258 --> 00:05:37.551
I started a lemonade stand.

00:05:37.551 --> 00:05:39.154
But I started five of them.

00:05:39.154 --> 00:05:43.397
I manipulated my friends to
stand behind the lemonade stands

00:05:43.397 --> 00:05:46.500
all day and what I did was 
I walked up and down the streets

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.878
of Edison, New Jersey, 
it was 1982 a different time,

00:05:49.878 --> 00:05:52.965
and I would watch cars drive.

00:05:52.965 --> 00:05:56.545
This is how sick and broken
of a human being I am, as a

00:05:56.545 --> 00:06:00.457
six-year-old I would walk up and
down the streets of New Jersey

00:06:00.457 --> 00:06:04.378
and I would sit, sit and watch
people drive on the street and

00:06:04.378 --> 00:06:09.716
try to figure out which tree or
pole was better for me to put my

00:06:09.716 --> 00:06:13.637
lemonade stand sign on because
I was following their eyes.

00:06:13.637 --> 00:06:17.516
And basically the rest of my
life has been one hard core

00:06:17.516 --> 00:06:20.269
religion around attention.

00:06:20.269 --> 00:06:21.979
I don't care about
traditional or digital.

00:06:21.979 --> 00:06:24.564
I stand here today and a lot of
people know me and I think I'm

00:06:24.564 --> 00:06:27.317
all about Facebook and Snapchat
and Instagram and I am but

00:06:27.317 --> 00:06:29.945
I will stand here today, 
my long-term plan, I built an

00:06:29.945 --> 00:06:32.862
agency from 30 to 800 people 
in the last five years from

00:06:32.862 --> 00:06:34.283
$3 to $100 million in revenue.

00:06:34.283 --> 00:06:38.704
No M&amp;amp;A, all growth but my main
goal is predicated on building a

00:06:38.704 --> 00:06:42.165
private equity firm on top of
it because while great companies

00:06:42.165 --> 00:06:44.725
like the one the presented right
before me can only spend money

00:06:44.725 --> 00:06:48.020
on their 7 to 12 monster brands,
there's ungodly amounts of great

00:06:48.020 --> 00:06:51.129
brands in that portfolio
that are going to be sold off.

00:06:51.129 --> 00:06:52.487
I'm going to buy those brands

00:06:52.487 --> 00:06:54.131
and I'm gonna run 
them through my machine.

00:06:54.131 --> 00:06:56.800
That is my plan of how I am
going buy the New York Jets.

00:06:56.800 --> 00:06:57.467
Right?

00:06:57.467 --> 00:06:58.447
That's my strategy.

00:06:58.447 --> 00:07:01.473
(audience laughter and applause)

00:07:01.473 --> 00:07:04.793
Not so fun right now.

00:07:04.793 --> 00:07:07.689
So for me what's been
interesting about building my

00:07:07.689 --> 00:07:11.693
agency is that we're not so
special, we're not so great.

00:07:11.693 --> 00:07:14.780
What we are is we are
disproportionately aligned with

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:17.491
our clients because 
I want to become our clients.

00:07:17.491 --> 00:07:21.370
My goal is not to run on the
highest margin so I can sell my

00:07:21.370 --> 00:07:25.040
agency for eight times EBITA
to a fucking holding company.

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.836
My goal is to build an 
actual business and

00:07:28.836 --> 00:07:31.755
what that's done is--
(audience applause)

00:07:31.755 --> 00:07:33.841
- Thank you.

00:07:33.841 --> 00:07:37.594
So what it's done is it's made
me want to break and rewire my

00:07:37.594 --> 00:07:40.264
first corporate
America meeting of my life.

00:07:40.264 --> 00:07:43.517
So in 2006 and '07 I did
something super smart.

00:07:43.517 --> 00:07:44.685
I invested in Facebook and

00:07:44.685 --> 00:07:47.146
Twitter very early on 
and then Tumblr.

00:07:47.146 --> 00:07:48.605
And then Uber.

00:07:48.605 --> 00:07:51.233
I'm mainly saying 
that to humblebrag but

00:07:51.233 --> 00:07:53.235
it's an important thing.

00:07:53.235 --> 00:07:56.446
I decided that this one pitch
that I had, because I built

00:07:56.446 --> 00:08:00.075
Wine Library on email marketing,
then the day Google AdWords came

00:08:00.075 --> 00:08:03.370
out I bought a word wine for
five cents a click before they

00:08:03.370 --> 00:08:06.999
raised the minimum to 10 cents a
click and I sold a lot of wine.

00:08:06.999 --> 00:08:09.543
As a matter of fact, it's
the great regret of my life.

00:08:09.543 --> 00:08:12.379
It is why I will pound you at
some point in my 18 minutes here

00:08:12.379 --> 00:08:14.256
on really understanding what's

00:08:14.256 --> 00:08:16.925
happening in the 
Facebook ad ecosystem.

00:08:16.925 --> 00:08:21.388
It is the 100% replication of
early Google and I was selling a

00:08:21.388 --> 00:08:24.933
ton of wine but I should of
put all my money into it because

00:08:24.933 --> 00:08:27.144
when you have a once in a
generation arbitrage of

00:08:27.144 --> 00:08:29.771
attention that is grossly
underpriced by the market,

00:08:29.771 --> 00:08:33.108
you need to become pot-committed
to extract all the value.

00:08:33.108 --> 00:08:35.985
And even though I grew my dad's
business from $3 to $60 million,

00:08:35.985 --> 00:08:38.654
the truth is and this is not fun
for me to say to you, it should

00:08:38.654 --> 00:08:41.490
have been $3 to $150 million
because all that direct mail

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:44.036
money and all that billboard
money and all that

00:08:44.036 --> 00:08:47.623
New York Times money should have
went all-in to Google because

00:08:47.623 --> 00:08:50.584
I would extracted such
phenomenal ROI.

00:08:50.584 --> 00:08:51.585
My career took an interesting

00:08:51.585 --> 00:08:54.171
turn four months after
YouTube came out.

00:08:54.171 --> 00:08:56.798
YouTube came out, I took a look
at it, it was another moment in

00:08:56.798 --> 00:09:00.427
my life like e-com, like email
marketing, like Google AdWords

00:09:00.427 --> 00:09:01.303
where I felt it.

00:09:01.303 --> 00:09:03.021
I was like this is
going to be big.

00:09:03.021 --> 00:09:06.086
And I started a wine show
called Wine Library TV.

00:09:06.086 --> 00:09:07.232
It was the first time that

00:09:07.232 --> 00:09:09.519
advertising wasn't 
driving my revenue.

00:09:09.519 --> 00:09:11.396
It was content.

00:09:11.396 --> 00:09:12.773
I was producing a show.

00:09:12.773 --> 00:09:14.191
The show was pretty basic.

00:09:14.191 --> 00:09:17.555
I sat in front of a camera
for 20 minutes and

00:09:17.555 --> 00:09:19.392
drank four bottles of wine.

00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:21.057
(audience laughter)

00:09:21.057 --> 00:09:23.705
Best gig I ever had.

00:09:23.705 --> 00:09:26.124
And at its height hundreds of
thousands of people a day were

00:09:26.124 --> 00:09:29.628
watching it and I was
selling lots and lots of wine.

00:09:29.628 --> 00:09:31.880
And that taught what I've always
known and I want to say this

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:34.341
before I go into the next part
of my rant because if I don't

00:09:34.341 --> 00:09:36.593
say it, it will get lost.

00:09:36.593 --> 00:09:38.678
My absolute belief is that

00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:41.848
creative is the 
variable of success.

00:09:41.848 --> 00:09:44.559
That the creative is
the variable of success.

00:09:44.559 --> 00:09:47.896
The problem is I fundamentally
do not believe how this

00:09:47.896 --> 00:09:51.024
marketplace brings
creative to life.

00:09:51.024 --> 00:09:55.862
If you go home and tell a
non-marketing person this story

00:09:55.862 --> 00:09:58.156
I want to know their reaction.

00:09:58.156 --> 00:09:59.699
Hey, Sally, best friend,

00:09:59.699 --> 00:10:01.576
let me actually tell you 
about my business.

00:10:01.576 --> 00:10:05.372
This is now the general
industry, no agency, no brand.

00:10:05.372 --> 00:10:07.415
This is the net
score and you know it.

00:10:07.415 --> 00:10:08.833
Here's the bottom line.

00:10:08.833 --> 00:10:12.796
Here is how most
brands work in 2017.

00:10:12.796 --> 00:10:14.962
We're there already, right?

00:10:14.962 --> 00:10:18.689
A human being, and one or two of
her contemporaries come up with

00:10:18.689 --> 00:10:21.257
some creative ideas for a brand.

00:10:21.257 --> 00:10:23.926
They sit in a room, they get
some insights, they have some

00:10:23.926 --> 00:10:27.304
strategy but in general
sometimes, by the way, they come

00:10:27.304 --> 00:10:29.390
up with creative ideas.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:31.878
Humans.
Three, five, six, seven.

00:10:31.881 --> 00:10:36.189
They then narrow down their five
to seven ideas to one to two,

00:10:36.189 --> 00:10:39.734
maybe three, because, you know,
the meeting to pitch this only

00:10:39.734 --> 00:10:41.403
has a certain amount of time.

00:10:41.403 --> 00:10:45.699
The person on the other end is
normally, by high percentage,

00:10:45.699 --> 00:10:48.869
somebody who went to business
school and was taught to run a

00:10:48.869 --> 00:10:51.163
P&amp;amp;L and a business.

00:10:51.163 --> 00:10:55.500
That person sits in a room,
lets the other person present.

00:10:55.500 --> 00:10:58.628
They watch a couple of videos,
talk about other cockamamie

00:10:58.628 --> 00:11:03.675
horse shit and then those 
two people at the top,

00:11:04.372 --> 00:11:06.498
the brand person and the

00:11:06.498 --> 00:11:08.201
creative person 
from that agency,

00:11:08.201 --> 00:11:13.498
those two human beings decide
what the creative is the brand.

00:11:13.498 --> 00:11:15.093
Now what's interesting 
about that creative is

00:11:15.093 --> 00:11:17.583
it has to be 100% vanilla.

00:11:17.583 --> 00:11:18.417
You know why?

00:11:18.417 --> 00:11:23.088
Because when they deploy that
creative 60 to 70% of the money,

00:11:23.088 --> 00:11:26.967
maybe 50 but probably closer to
70 if you factor in the other

00:11:26.967 --> 00:11:31.180
channels besides television,
but 50% of that money is spent

00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:35.184
in the form of making 
a 30 second video.

00:11:35.184 --> 00:11:39.623
That 30 second video in 2017 is
deployed the following way;

00:11:39.623 --> 00:11:43.740
it plays in-between television.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:45.579
Linear television.

00:11:45.579 --> 00:11:49.400
Let's get really honest
here for one second, friends.

00:11:49.400 --> 00:11:53.713
By show by show of hands, and
remember lying is the devil,--

00:11:53.713 --> 00:11:55.053
(audience laughter)

00:11:55.053 --> 00:12:00.058
by show of hands, how many
people here in late 2016 when

00:12:00.058 --> 00:12:04.682
they watch television, 
outside of live sports,

00:12:04.682 --> 00:12:07.268
watch it on your time?

00:12:07.268 --> 00:12:09.104
Not when it actually airs.

00:12:09.104 --> 00:12:13.697
DVR, TiVo, on your
time, raise your hand.

00:12:13.697 --> 00:12:15.465
Oh, weird, everybody.

00:12:15.465 --> 00:12:17.376
(audience laughter)

00:12:17.376 --> 00:12:20.629
